{"post_stream":{"posts":[{"id":816,"name":"Matthias Springer","username":"MSpringer","avatar_template":"/letter_avatar_proxy/v4/letter/m/e274bd/{size}.png","created_at":"2024-06-05T09:12:26.288Z","cooked":"\u003cp\u003e\u003cimg src=\"https://discourse.igelsociety.org/images/emoji/twitter/speech_balloon.png?v=12\" title=\":speech_balloon:\" class=\"emoji\" alt=\":speech_balloon:\"\u003e \u003cstrong\u003eSpeaker\u003c/strong\u003e: Matthias Springer \u003ca class=\"mention\" href=\"/u/mspringer\"\u003e@MSpringer\u003c/a\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cimg src=\"https://discourse.igelsociety.org/images/emoji/twitter/classical_building.png?v=12\" title=\":classical_building:\" class=\"emoji\" alt=\":classical_building:\"\u003e \u003cstrong\u003eAffiliation\u003c/strong\u003e: Ludwig-Maximlians-University Munich\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eTitle\u003c/strong\u003e: \u003cem\u003eLiterariness as a Linguistic Figure\u003c/em\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cblockquote\u003e\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eAbstract\u003c/strong\u003e (long version below): Literariness is primarily based on deliberately shaped language, thereby transforming a narration will become a literary narration. In my experiment an originally literary narrated episode is passed on by retelling it in three times. The hypothesis is that the narrative content within the episode is preserved in these retellings, while the literary content as a measure of literariness, for which language is largely responsible, is lost.\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003c/blockquote\u003e\n\u003cbr\u003e\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cimg src=\"https://discourse.igelsociety.org/images/emoji/twitter/newspaper.png?v=12\" title=\":newspaper:\" class=\"emoji\" alt=\":newspaper:\"\u003e \u003cstrong\u003eLong abstract\u003c/strong\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003eMy contribution is based on the thesis that literariness is primarily created by deliberately moulding und shaping language, thereby transforming a narration will become a literary narration. To investigate this, an experiment is conducted in which originally literary narrated episode is passed on by retelling it in three times. The hypothesis is that the narrative content within the episode is preserved, if not sharpened, in these retellings, while the literary content as a measure of literariness, for which language is largely responsible in Skolvskij´s and Jakobson´s sense, is lost. This can be observed in the constructions used in the retellings, which come ever closer to usage-based language. Literariness is measured by the content of literary information in the original narration and its retellings on the basis of the likelihood that these constructions have in usage-based language.\u003cbr\u003e\nFinally, this study can make further contribution to the validity of language-based theories of literariness and help to distinguish them from AI generated art.\u003c/p\u003e","post_number":1,"post_type":1,"updated_at":"2024-06-30T05:32:44.339Z","reply_count":0,"reply_to_post_number":null,"quote_count":0,"incoming_link_count":8,"reads":26,"readers_count":25,"score":45.2,"yours":false,"topic_id":534,"topic_slug":"literariness-as-a-linguistic-figure","display_username":"Matthias Springer","primary_group_name":null,"flair_name":null,"flair_url":null,"flair_bg_color":null,"flair_color":null,"version":3,"can_edit":false,"can_delete":false,"can_recover":false,"can_wiki":false,"link_counts":[{"url":"https://discourse.igelsociety.org/t/igel-2025-conference-schedule/527","internal":true,"reflection":true,"title":"IGEL 2024 conference schedule","clicks":0}],"read":true,"user_title":null,"bookmarked":false,"actions_summary":[],"moderator":false,"admin":false,"staff":false,"user_id":149,"hidden":false,"trust_level":2,"deleted_at":null,"user_deleted":false,"edit_reason":"Ownership transferred","can_view_edit_history":true,"wiki":false,"event":null,"calendar_details":[],"can_accept_answer":false,"can_unaccept_answer":false,"accepted_answer":false,"can_vote":false},{"id":1009,"name":"Frank Hakemulder","username":"Hakemulder","avatar_template":"/letter_avatar_proxy/v4/letter/h/898d66/{size}.png","created_at":"2024-07-16T08:14:02.808Z","cooked":"\u003cp\u003eInteresting experiment Matthias. It reminds me of a book entitled… Remembering by \u003ca href=\"https://archive.org/details/Bartlett1932Remembering\" rel=\"noopener nofollow ugc\"\u003eBartlett\u003c/a\u003e. One of his methods was similar to yours: retelling stories. What he found was that strange/unfamiliar elements were “neutralized” in the retelling, made more “normal”. Would the same hold for stylistic deviations?\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003eOne might also expect that because of the strangeness/strikingness such elements might be remembered better.  Curious what you think and what you believe the implications are of your study for our field, for foregrounding theories in particular. See you soon!\u003c/p\u003e","post_number":2,"post_type":1,"updated_at":"2024-07-16T08:14:02.808Z","reply_count":0,"reply_to_post_number":null,"quote_count":0,"incoming_link_count":0,"reads":15,"readers_count":14,"score":3.0,"yours":false,"topic_id":534,"topic_slug":"literariness-as-a-linguistic-figure","display_username":"Frank Hakemulder","primary_group_name":null,"flair_name":null,"flair_url":null,"flair_bg_color":null,"flair_color":null,"version":1,"can_edit":false,"can_delete":false,"can_recover":false,"can_wiki":false,"link_counts":[{"url":"https://archive.org/details/Bartlett1932Remembering","internal":false,"reflection":false,"title":"Bartlett 1932 Remembering : Free Download, Borrow, and Streaming : Internet Archive","clicks":0}],"read":true,"user_title":null,"bookmarked":false,"actions_summary":[],"moderator":false,"admin":false,"staff":false,"user_id":91,"hidden":false,"trust_level":2,"deleted_at":null,"user_deleted":false,"edit_reason":null,"can_view_edit_history":true,"wiki":false,"event":null,"can_accept_answer":false,"can_unaccept_answer":false,"accepted_an